          "text": "{\"relation\":\"DIVERGENT\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Elders broadly denote mature, representative leaders of God’s covenant community. In Revelation, the leading identification is representatives of redeemed humanity, although heavenly-council beings are a recognized competing interpretation.\",\"book_summary\":\"Elders are leaders of heavenly assemblies who form a court around God’s throne and are specifically identified with constellations and their stars.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Representative leaders of the covenant people, with Revelation’s elders most often identified as representatives of the redeemed.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Celestial court members identified as constellations and stars within the host of heaven.\",\"core_relation\":\"CONTRADICTS\",\"extension_relation\":\"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book shifts the referent from human or redeemed representatives to celestial entities and therefore excludes ordinary Israelite and church elders, as well as the predominant redeemed-human identification of Revelation’s elders. Its heavenly-court setting overlaps a recognized alternative, but its identification with constellations is more specific.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"In the same apocalyptic context, identifying the elders as constellations or stars replaces the consensus’s leading identification of them as redeemed human representatives. These are distinct referents unless a secondary layered symbolism is independently established.\",\"rationale\":\"The shared language of leadership, representation, assembly, and enthroned court does not eliminate the ontological difference at the core. The book aligns partially with the listed heavenly/divine-council alternative, so it is not wholly novel, but it goes further by identifying the council members as constellations. That materially replaces the consensus’s central redeemed-community referent rather than merely adjusting its boundaries.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The cited throne-room and heavenly-assembly texts support a celestial court, but none of the printed passages explicitly identifies the elders with constellations or stars. “Host of heaven” and “assembly of the saints” can instead denote angelic beings, while Matthew 19:28 directly associates enthroned judges with human apostles.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
